4. STRICT ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL

Implement three roles:

1. Master Admin
2. Secondary Admin / Manager
3. Staff

Enforce permissions in the backend and database layer, not merely by hiding buttons in React.

Master Admin

The initial master admin is the highest-privilege account.

Only the master admin can:

- Create, edit, deactivate, and manage secondary admins.
- Create, edit, deactivate, and manage staff accounts.
- Assign staff to secondary admins.
- Configure which staff and financial modules each secondary admin may access.
- View and edit all staff attendance, reports, rates, templates, charges, adjustments, and payment statuses.
- Approve or reject attendance when authorized as the master admin.
- View all administrator-managed financial ledgers.
- View all secondary-admin payment records and their associated staff data.
- Access the complete audit history.
- Manage global settings and role permissions.

The master admin must not accidentally lose master privileges through an ordinary role edit. Master-admin reassignment, if supported, must require an explicit protected operation.

Secondary Admin / Manager

A secondary admin must have restricted, configurable access.

The master admin must be able to assign:

- Which staff members this admin manages.
- Which staff reports this admin can view.
- Which staff attendance this admin can approve or reject.
- Which staff records this admin can edit.
- Which additional financial modules this admin can access.
- Whether this admin may add, edit, or only view financial entries in assigned modules.

Secondary admins must not automatically see every staff member or every financial ledger.

They must not create additional admins or change their own permissions.

Every request must verify the secondary admin's actual assigned scope on the server.

Staff

